    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "236b1072_ded744c7",
        "filename": "vmbase/entry.S",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 191,
      "author": {
        "id": 1986725
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-30T08:55:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Both SMC32 and SMC64 calls use `w0` for the first parameter (function ID). So it makes no difference for the call, but allows us to use the `mov_i` macro to write the function ID to the register. The same pattern is already present in `reset_or_hang` macro in `common.h`.",
      "parentUuid": "204e77b7_35f0b94c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 191,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 191,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "4de6abc5a4cf41ede214e559b31a61a159171264",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2e6c50db_4fa29f90",
        "filename": "vmbase/entry.S",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 191,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-31T10:39:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, exactly; the FID is always a 32-bit value, even on 64-bit systems and using `w0` allows the assembler to catch a potential error. In this case, `mov_i` is a bit overkill because it\u0027ll result in `movk w0, 0` but I\u0027ll resolve to avoid spending more time on optimizing out 2 instructions.\n\n\u003e The same pattern is already present in `reset_or_hang` macro in `common.h`.\n\nI wouldn\u0027t trust the guy that wrote that :^)",
      "parentUuid": "236b1072_ded744c7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 191,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 191,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "4de6abc5a4cf41ede214e559b31a61a159171264",
